WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.600
Speaker 1: Today, I'm going to be speaking
with Matthew Stewart, who is a philosopher

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:09.380
and author of several books, including most
recently, The 9.9 percent the new aristocracy

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:13.929
that is entrenching inequality and warping
our culture.

00:00:13.929 --> 00:00:15.210
Really appreciate having you on.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.369
Matthew, thank you.

00:00:16.369 --> 00:00:19.460
Speaker 5: Yeah, it's great to be here, David,
thanks for having me.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:24.530
Speaker 1: So we often hear about the one
percent we hear about, the 0.1 percent we

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:30.220
hear about the bottom 90 percent many times
or the bottom 50 percent in discussions of

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:33.020
economics and society and culture.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:38.110
What is significant about this nine point
nine percent that made you want to study and

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:40.450
write about it specifically?

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:47.470
Speaker 5: So let's not forget that the 0.1
percent is actually deriving the wealth inequality

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:50.760
and the bottom 90 percent is the group that's
losing.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:53.910
So the there's two interesting things about
the nine point nine percent.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.460
One is that it's actually holding on to its
wealth.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.670
So that's, I think, really important.

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:04.140
And then the other thing is that it plays
a much bigger role in driving our our values

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.750
and our culture.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:09.420
So that's I think, what makes them especially
interesting because I think we're all part

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.240
of it, even if we're not financially part
of it.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.140
Speaker 1: Can you talk about the ways in
which that group drives culture?

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:22.000
Speaker 5: Yeah, I mean, so, for example,
in education is pretty straightforward.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.420
And the plan for making it into the nine point
nine percent today is to is to go to college

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:32.410
and go to a good college and get a good degree
and so on.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:37.671
And that kind of plan made a lot of sense
when college was inexpensive, when a lot of

00:01:37.671 --> 00:01:42.890
it was publicly paid for and when there were
jobs for people who got those kind of degrees.

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:45.360
But all of those realities are gone.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.500
And yet the value system is, if anything,
even more entrenched.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:52.440
So there's even more emphasis on you can make
it if you just go to the right college and

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.350
sort of figure out how the system works.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.860
And then it turns out, of course, that the
people who succeed in that or are the ones

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.460
who already start with a leg up whose parents
were already in the nine point nine percent.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:07.440
Speaker 1: What are the economic dispute?

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.069
What's the line for entering that group that
nine point nine percent in terms of where

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:16.740
is the 90th percentile in terms of income
and wealth and how far up does it reach?

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:17.819
Speaker 5: Yeah.

00:02:17.819 --> 00:02:22.310
So but let me also just qualify and say that,
you know, we don't have to fetishize about

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:23.470
the numbers because they move.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:29.590
And also, it's not just about the numbers,
but relative proportions, but you know, basically

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:35.340
you need about $1.2 million in net worth to
make it into the top 10.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:43.599
That's our 9.9 percent of the wealth distribution,
and your income should be in the 120000 plus

00:02:43.599 --> 00:02:44.599
range.

00:02:44.599 --> 00:02:46.490
So that's what kind of gets you into it financially.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:50.860
And then at the top end, obviously, it's it's
a it's different as you get to the top at

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:59.130
about 20 million will push you at the top
now in wealth and, you know, sort of 500000

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:01.709
or so in income, which, you know, sounds sounds
like a lot.

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:05.210
But in fact, when you compare it to the very
large sums that are going into the 0.1%, it's

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:06.210
actually pretty small.

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:08.810
So to Europe is more homogenous than people
like to think.

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:15.209
Speaker 1: Now what's interesting is certainly,
I would guess, the vast majority of the bottom

00:03:15.209 --> 00:03:18.990
half of that group would consider themselves
middle class.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:24.470
And many who live in the most expensive cities
would would absolutely insist that that they

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.920
are middle class is that part.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:32.660
Is that culturally relevant that so much of
that group considers itself middle class?

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:34.860
And is it accurate in some sense?

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:40.980
Speaker 5: Yeah, so what's happening is is
the hollowing out of the middle class, but

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:45.989
the ideas are about, you know, 20 or 30 years
behind the economic realities.

00:03:45.989 --> 00:03:53.480
So a lot of people in this 9.9 percent, even
in the bottom part, they don't get how wealthy

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:55.720
they are relative to the rest of society.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:04:00.480
They don't quite get how, how much better
they do have it.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.660
And of course, part of the reason why I don't
get it is because they are also being stressed.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.420
I mean, it's pretty hard to stay in that group.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:10.110
You have to work really hard and, you know,
get some lucky breaks and be in the right

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:11.470
place at the right time and so on.

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:18.370
So I think there's a significant misperception
in the group, which is how old they are.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:19.969
And a lot of that shows up in surveys.

00:04:19.969 --> 00:04:23.983
You ask people, you know, how much to the
people at the top make and they say, Oh, well,

00:04:23.983 --> 00:04:25.900
you know, the average CEO makes a million
dollars a year.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:27.270
And of course, that's that's preposterous.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:32.009
And the average CEO makes 17 or 18 million
dollars a year for top 500 companies.

00:04:32.009 --> 00:04:38.259
So I think a lot of misperceptions and the
when you mentioned the middle class illusion

00:04:38.259 --> 00:04:39.740
is is really big one.

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:43.360
Speaker 1: Can you talk a little bit about
the racial makeup of this group that you're

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:44.360
describing, generally speaking?

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:48.270
Speaker 5: Yeah, I mean, pretty simply, it's
mostly white.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:54.980
I mean, it's about 90 percent white as against,
you know, and take measure at 65 to 70 percent

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:57.210
of the general population.

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:03.810
There definitely are people of color in in
the nine point nine percent a significant

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:04.810
group.

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:09.110
But in relative to the representation in the
total population, it's significantly lower.

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:15.619
So I think the Black shares about one point
seven percent of the nine point nine percent,

00:05:15.619 --> 00:05:20.370
as against about 13 percent of the general
population Latinos roughly similar

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.240
Speaker 1: in terms of political power.

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:28.529
I mean, we understand the dynamics of money
and political power in the United States,

00:05:28.529 --> 00:05:36.059
generally speaking, but that top 9.9 percent
is much bigger in terms of population by definition

00:05:36.059 --> 00:05:39.389
than the top 0.1 percent in terms of aggregate
power.

00:05:39.389 --> 00:05:43.550
And that doesn't mean it's being wielded effectively,
but in terms of aggregate power.

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:48.419
What is the power dynamic between that top
0.1 percent and the following nine point nine?

00:05:48.419 --> 00:05:55.469
Speaker 5: You know, it's it's the nine point
nine percent presents this big political problem,

00:05:55.469 --> 00:06:01.689
especially for the Democratic Party, frankly,
because that group now is leaning blue.

00:06:01.689 --> 00:06:06.719
It's not completely blue, but it's more so
than the general population, whereas the top

00:06:06.719 --> 00:06:11.810
0.1 percent, and notwithstanding all the nonsense
about limousine liberals actually functionally

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:15.020
leans very much to the right.

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:19.319
And and the dynamic between them, it's a really
great question.

00:06:19.319 --> 00:06:24.360
It's, you know, on the one hand, 9.4 percent
is fundamentally dependent on the 0.1 percent

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:26.189
economically.

00:06:26.189 --> 00:06:32.189
But on the other hand, culturally, there is
a big gap and a significant part.

00:06:32.189 --> 00:06:38.059
Shifting left is aware of that and tries to
pull away the big danger for the Democratic

00:06:38.059 --> 00:06:45.419
Party and the left is that this group, which
is, I think has a disproportionate weight

00:06:45.419 --> 00:06:53.550
in left politics, doesn't necessarily understand
or share the interests of the other 90 percent

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:54.629
of the population.

00:06:54.629 --> 00:07:01.379
So they tend to skew of the left in ways that
can be ultimately go against the interests

00:07:01.379 --> 00:07:04.429
of the large part of the population and sort
of work indirectly for the top.

00:07:04.429 --> 00:07:05.999
Speaker 1: Oh, that's interesting.

00:07:05.999 --> 00:07:07.619
So let's see if we can think about that a
little.

00:07:07.619 --> 00:07:15.659
So you've got so the top 0.1 percent is leans
overtly to the Republican side, and it's sort

00:07:15.659 --> 00:07:21.599
of clear that the economic interests of that
group in terms of the policy they want is

00:07:21.599 --> 00:07:24.259
not good for the bottom 90 percent.

00:07:24.259 --> 00:07:28.179
It sounds like you're saying with this the
following nine point nine percent.

00:07:28.179 --> 00:07:33.319
Nominally, these are folks who identify more
as Democrats than Republicans and believe

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:39.150
themselves to lean more left than they lean
right, but maybe without even really knowing

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:40.150
it.

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:44.580
The policies they want or the status quo that
they reinforce is also not good for that bottom

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:45.580
90 percent.

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:53.720
Speaker 5: Yeah, I mean, a classic example
for me is is the real estate game, so we don't

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:57.710
actually have a lot of attention to this issue
in national politics, but it actually drives

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:03.729
a lot of our culture and it drives a lot of
the issues that we have this huge segregation,

00:08:03.729 --> 00:08:06.219
economic and racial in our neighborhoods.

00:08:06.219 --> 00:08:09.999
And the role of the nine point nine percent
in that, I would say on aggregate is really

00:08:09.999 --> 00:08:14.599
pretty regressive because what it ends up
doing is is saying, Oh, the issue isn't, you

00:08:14.599 --> 00:08:15.599
know, our neighborhoods.

00:08:15.599 --> 00:08:21.729
The issue is has to do with any number of
other things, you know, personal prejudices,

00:08:21.729 --> 00:08:26.209
with education, with economic policies that
we're interested just more.

00:08:26.209 --> 00:08:32.740
But in fact, the 9.9 percent tends to come
down very heavily in favor of very restrictive

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:39.240
zoning and other very restrictive land use
policies that drive up the price of housing

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:43.810
that have created an affordability housing
crisis that reinforce segregation dynamics

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:47.480
that reinforce educational segregation dynamics.

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:51.160
And so they end up being very regressive,
and it's something that the nine point nine

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:52.160
percent.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:58.800
I think intellectually, the more self-aware
members are are onto this, but functionally

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:00.600
the way they actually act.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:06.550
And so, you know, reproducing these these
divisions that in turn increase inequality

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:08.590
and work to the benefit of the 0.1 percent.

00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:13.420
Speaker 1: One of the things that often comes
up in conversation with people who do well

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:17.430
but are not in the top 0.1 percent and maybe
not even in the top one percent.

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:23.140
So maybe we're talking about 90th to 98th
percentile to kind of imagine again, you know,

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:27.910
we're talking about these are not exact, exact
delineations, but there's often the feeling

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:34.750
among the 90 to 98 percent that, hey, the
very top, it doesn't pay enough in taxes and

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:38.110
we do need to do things for the bottom 90
percent.

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:43.950
But also the taxes are a little too high on
us, the 90 to the 98.

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:50.650
Can you talk a little bit about that dynamic
and and really where some of these lines should

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:52.170
be in terms of taxation?

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:53.710
Speaker 5: Yeah.

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:56.010
So there's a grain of truth in that.

00:09:56.010 --> 00:10:00.220
It turns out that the the real tax rate, when
you throw it all in the federal, state and

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:03.340
local, you look at capital and income and
so on.

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:05.870
The real tax rate for the at the very top
is lower.

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:11.240
It's it's dramatically an absurdly lower than
it is for the upper middle class and so on.

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:17.180
So vis-a-vis the 0.1 percent, yeah, the 9.9
percent is getting screwed like the rest of

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:18.390
society.

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:21.320
There's another side of it, though that's
a little harder for people the nine point

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:22.520
nine percent to grasp.

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:28.600
And that's that our tax system is so arcane
and complicated that it ends up indirectly

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:30.420
subsidizing a lot of people invisibly.

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:35.690
So, you know, a classic case in point is the
mortgage interest deduction.

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:41.911
But there are many other bits and pieces like
that that have the effect of subsidizing the

00:10:41.911 --> 00:10:45.170
nine point nine percent in ways that are kind
of invisible.

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:51.260
So people in that group think that their taxes
are actually higher than they are another

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:52.270
going back to the real estate.

00:10:52.270 --> 00:10:54.190
That's another kind of invisible subsidy we
have.

00:10:54.190 --> 00:11:01.380
We notwithstanding the fact that we do have
property, municipal property taxes, we basically

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:05.280
don't capture the implicit rental value of
real estate.

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:09.510
And so people who own their own homes end
up getting an indirect tax subsidy, but they're

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:11.410
completely unaware of this.

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:15.030
They think they're geniuses for having bought
into real estate market when they did.

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:18.600
And so they're kind of unaware that some of
the money that's coming their way is really

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:20.300
an indirect tax subsidy.

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:22.930
So it's a really complicated picture.

00:11:22.930 --> 00:11:25.620
And of course, I'm sure you'd agree if we
can wave magic wand we replace the current

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:30.230
tax system have something radically more progressive
and a lot more fair.

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:35.140
Speaker 1: Yeah, I've had very interesting
conversations with folks who say, you know,

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:40.200
I don't really make that much and I'm touching
the 33 percent federal tax bracket.

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:44.060
And then when I live, if I live in New York
or California or Massachusetts, then you know,

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:46.920
you're throwing five to five to 10 percent
on top of that.

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:49.750
And I'll often say to these folks, Listen,
you don't have to tell me exactly what you

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:55.110
make but understand that you're touching that
top 33 percent.

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:58.650
But not all of your income is taxed at that
because we have, you know, these are these

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:04.231
are brackets, marginal tax brackets, and then
you've got your standard deduction and you

00:12:04.231 --> 00:12:07.060
have a mortgage and you've got, you know,
all these different things.

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:12.860
You should really look at that first page
of your 10:40, which says total tax, divide

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:17.100
it by your income and you are going to find
a shockingly low number.

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:22.110
And many times it's 17 18 percent federally
overall.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:27.380
And these are folks absolutely in that 9.9
percent who themselves don't really have an

00:12:27.380 --> 00:12:30.460
understanding of the degree to which they
are paying federal taxes.

00:12:30.460 --> 00:12:35.880
Speaker 5: Yeah, but but let's just not forget
that, I mean, you know, the big money is at

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:36.990
the very top.

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:40.760
Of course, and the big question here absolutely
is actually to go after this.

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:41.760
Speaker 1: Yeah, yeah.

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:46.840
Another two or three points for someone making
one hundred and $80000 a year has almost no

00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:50.500
impact compared to what we're talking about
in the 0.1 percent, without a doubt.

00:12:50.500 --> 00:12:51.530
Speaker 5: Right, right.

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:54.600
But then let me also mention on the other
end, because this is something that happens

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:58.860
when we talk about the 99 percent we forget
about people that are at the at the other

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:01.130
end of the economic spectrum.

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:03.060
We don't think about sales tax is the big
issue.

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:06.810
Generally, if you remember the 99 percent,
but that's actually a pretty significant hit

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:10.620
to a lot of people with lower incomes.

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:14.710
We don't think about the the fight, the, you
know, the the payroll tax deduction, but that,

00:13:14.710 --> 00:13:18.660
you know, if you've got it, if you've got
a minimum wage job, that's a big, big chunk

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:19.660
of your income.

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:25.370
So things are much more regressive than than
people understand in the 9.9 percent.

00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:28.090
Speaker 1: Without question, the book is fascinating.

00:13:28.090 --> 00:13:32.760
It's called the nine point nine percent the
new aristocracy that is entrenching inequality

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:34.400
and warping our culture.

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:37.860
We've been speaking with the book's author,
Matthew Stewart.

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:39.110
Really appreciate your time today.

00:13:39.110 --> 00:13:41.050
Speaker 5: Yeah, great to be here.

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:41.600
Thank you.

